# Musette in D major, BWV Anh. 126 (anonymous; formerly attributed to
# J. S. Bach)
#
# From the 1725 Notebook for Anna Magdalena Bach (No. 22), one of the
# pieces of doubtful attribution; the Notebook copy names no composer.
#
# Character: a musette imitates the French bagpipe: a drone bass (here
# octave Ds, then As, then Es, leaping in eighths) under a bright tune.
# The tune answers itself in two registers, high (bars 1-2, 5-6) then low,
# doubled by the bass (bars 3-4, 7-8). Lively and rustic.
#
# Time signature: 2/4, 20 bars in the Mutopia score and in the MIDI
# (8 + 12, each half repeated); the first note is on the downbeat.
#
# Form: da capo. The second half ends on A, the dominant (bar 20), and
# the fermata on the D at the end of bar 8 marks the Fine. So the piece
# is played A A B B A, ending at bar 8. The MIDI plays the 20 bars once;
# the repeats and the return are written out with `play`.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 92. Performed as above it is 48 bars, 96 quarters.
# MusicBrainz track times: Luc Beausejour (harpsichord) 1:01, about 94;
# Pieter-Jan Belder (harpsichord) 1:02, about 93; Rosalyn Tureck 1:02,
# about 93; Igor Kipnis (harpsichord) 0:57, about 101; Justin Taylor
# (harpsichord) 0:51, about 113; Mahan Esfahani 0:42, quicker still.
# These conversions assume the A A B B A form; I could not check which
# recordings take the da capo (without it the same times give about 80).
# 92 sits with Beausejour, Belder and Tureck: the drone leaps stay clear.
#
# Structure (my reading of the Mutopia score, after Bach-Gesellschaft):
#   1-4    the tune high (1-2), answered low with the bass (3-4), D major
#   5-8    the same again, closing on D with the fermata (bar 8)
#   9-12   second half on an A drone, A major colour
#   13-17  over an E drone: the tune's leaps with D-sharp (toward A major)
#   18-20  closing in A, the dominant, ready for the return
#
# Bars as performed (after `play`):
#   A 1-8, A repeat 9-16, B 17-28, B repeat 29-40, A da capo 41-48.
#
# The chip: the drone's low notes (D2, E2, A2) are below B2 and the
# converter lifts them an octave, so the octave leaps of the drone become
# repeated notes (66 notes lifted). The pulse of the drone survives; its
# leap does not. Nothing in this file can change that.
#
# Plan: terraced like the drones of the instrument: the high answers
# full, each repeat an echo, the da capo full again to close.

tempo 92
play 1-8 1-8 9-20 9-20 1-8

voice mid 1        # inner notes are passing chord tones; keep them back

dyn 1 f            # bars 1-8: A, full, bright
dyn 9 mp           # bars 9-16: A repeat, an echo
dyn 17 mf          # bars 17-28: B, on the A and E drones
dyn 29 mp          # bars 29-40: B repeat, an echo
dyn 41 f           # bars 41-48: A da capo, full to the Fine

broaden 8 0.9      # the fermata D at the end of A (and the breath before
broaden 16 0.9     # the B half); the same after the repeat
broaden 40 0.92    # end of B on the dominant: a breath before the da capo

rit 1 0.6          # bar 48: the fermata D at the Fine
